# Local Native Speech Commands

Uses the browser's `SpeechRecognition` API with `processLocally = true`.
Audio recognition and wake-word matching stay on-device; the app never falls
back to remote/browser-cloud recognition. The browser may download its own
local language pack before listening can start.

- **Wake word:** continuously compares recognized whole-word tokens with the
  configured wake phrase. The native recognizer receives a phrase-bias hint
  when the browser supports it.
- **AI trigger phrase:** configure one to six words (default `send`). Matching
  tolerates small spelling errors and adjacent transpositions, including when
  the phrase is split across recognition results. The trigger is displayed on
  the glasses but excluded from AI text. The rolling local history holds up to
  512 words.
- **Glasses:** after wake activation, buffers words and sends configurable
  chunks to Endpoint 1 as `POST /display` JSON (`{"text":"..."}`). Set the
  endpoint to the device route, defaulting to
  `http://192.168.31.180/display`. Each request uses centered text, top
  vertical alignment, font level 3, and scroll speed 10 to match the n8n
  example. Small trailing chunks flush after 700 ms without ending wake
  detection; the configurable minimum post interval defaults to 350 ms.
- **AI pipeline:** say the configured trigger phrase after the wake phrase.
  Endpoint 2 receives the configured number of most recent recognized words as JSON:
  `{"event":"voice_command","command":"send","wordCount":20,"text":"..."}`.

## Files
- `index.html` — UI, local speech recognition, wake-word matching, and endpoint relay
- `speech-text.mjs` — tokenization, normalization, and fuzzy trigger matching

No build step or npm install is needed.
Settings, including endpoints, language, wake/AI trigger words, and word
counts, are automatically saved in the browser's IndexedDB.

---

## Option A — Cloudflare Pages (what you're already using)

1. Push `index.html` to the root of your Pages project (or a subfolder).
2. Deploy as a static site — no build command needed.
3. Open the deployed `https://your-project.pages.dev` URL.
4. Set Endpoint 1 to the glasses' `/display` URL. Browsers may block an HTTPS
  page from calling the glasses over plain HTTP as mixed content; CORS support
  on the ESP32 does not override that restriction. Use a trusted HTTPS proxy
  that can reach the ESP32, or another browser-approved secure route to it.

## Option B — Self-host on your PC (serves over plain HTTP, zero mixed-content issues)

```bash
cd stt-app
python3 -m http.server 8080
```

Then open `http://localhost:8080` on the PC. For mobile microphone access, the
page must be served from a secure context (normally HTTPS); opening the PC's
plain-HTTP LAN address on a phone generally does not qualify. HTTPS hosting
also needs a secure route to the ESP32 because browsers can block HTTPS-to-HTTP
requests. Use a trusted local HTTPS proxy or another secure route that can
reach the device.

Use a desktop browser that supports on-device `SpeechRecognition` language
packs (currently Chromium-based desktop browsers with the local recognition
API). On first start, the app checks or installs the selected local language
pack. Microphone permission is requested immediately when you press Start, before
any language-pack download begins. Use HTTPS or localhost and keep the page visible; browser support for local
recognition and wake locks varies, and background tabs may still be suspended.
The native recognizer captures and preprocesses its own microphone audio; the
Web Speech API does not accept an externally preprocessed audio stream. A web
page cannot guarantee continuous recognition while backgrounded or suspended.
Many mobile browsers do not yet expose the local language-pack API. In those
browsers the app reports local STT as unsupported and does not silently use a
remote recognizer.

---

## Recognition behavior

Wake matching uses final native-recognition results, normalized into whole
word tokens so punctuation and capitalization do not prevent a match. The
application retains at most 512 recent recognized words locally. The wake
phrase and `send` command are excluded from the words sent to Endpoint 2.
Wake/send phrase bias is intentionally low to avoid making the recognizer
hallucinate the trigger words. Consecutive repeated speech tokens are capped
to two, and adjacent duplicates are collapsed in the AI payload. Results with
a reported confidence below the configurable threshold (default `0.25`) are
ignored; set it to `0` to disable confidence filtering. Browsers that do not
report a confidence value are not filtered by this setting.